The '''2026 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election''' took place on Thursday 7 May 2026, alongside [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections in the United Kingdom]]. All 63 members of [[Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council]] in [[South Yorkshire]] were elected following boundary changes.

This was the first election on an all-out cycle whereby all councillors will be elected every four years, after the change was approved by the council in 2025.<ref>{{cite web |title=Barnsley Council announces results of election cycle public consultation |url=https://www.barnsley.gov.uk/news/barnsley-council-announces-results-of-election-cycle-public-consultation/ |website=Barnsley Council |access-date=16 January 2026 |date=14 May 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

==Background==
Barnsley was created in 1974 as a [[metropolitan borough]]. Labour have always performed well in local elections, holding majority control of the council since its creation.<ref>{{cite news |title=BBC News - Election 2011 - England council elections - Barnsley |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/election2011/council/html/cc.stm |access-date=16 January 2026 |work=BBC News}}</ref> The closest Labour have come to losing their majority on the council was [[2006 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election|2006]] and [[2008 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election|2008]], where the success of the Barnsley Independents saw the party reduced to holding just over half the seats.<ref>{{cite web |title=Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council Election Results 1973-2012 |url=https://www.electionscentre.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Barnsley-1973-2012.pdf |website=Elections Centre |access-date=16 January 2026}}</ref> Since [[2012 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election|2012]], Labour have held over 70% of seats on the council.

The Barnsley Independents formed the principal opposition from 2006 to [[2018 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election|2018]], when the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] briefly became the second largest party. The [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] overtook the Conservatives in [[2019 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election|2019]] and have formed the principal opposition since.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour suffers "worst night in 15 years" in Barnsley local elections |url=https://www.yorkshirepost.co.uk/news/politics/labour-suffers-worst-night-in-15-years-in-barnsley-local-elections-39024 |access-date=16 January 2026 |work=Yorkshire Post |date=3 May 2019 |language=en}}</ref>

Prior to this election, Barnsley elected it councillors on a four year cycle, with one councillor elected from each three-member ward in each election. At the most recent election in 2024, [[2024 Barnsley Metropolitan Borough Council election|2024]], Labour won 17 seats, taking one from the Conservatives, but did not increase their majority due to losing a vacant seat last held by the party to the Liberal Democrats, which won 4 seats.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour retains grip on Barnsley Council |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ce9rn3lg0lro |access-date=16 January 2026 |work=BBC News |date=3 May 2024}}</ref> Following boundary changes, the 2026 election will use a new set of ward boundaries.<ref>[https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2025/460/made The Barnsley (Electoral Changes) Order 2025]</ref>

